{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And HaShem spoke, etc. — and they shall take for Me a contribution, etc.</b> Three contributions are mentioned here, etc. And there are several questions about this matter. For why attribute the first two contributions to HaShem, blessed be He — for in the first it says ״וְיִקְחוּ לִי״, 'for Me,' and in the second it says ״תִּקְחוּ אֶת תְּרוּמָתִי״, 'My contribution' — but in the third He mentioned neither one nor the other, and on the contrary it says in it ״מֵאִתָּם״, attributing it to the donors? And furthermore one may ask: why in the two first contributions did He put the term קִיחָה, 'taking,' before the term תְּרוּמָה, 'contribution,' whereas in the third He put תְּרוּמָה before לְקִיחָה? And furthermore one may ask: how could He insert the statement ״מֵאֵת כָּל אִישׁ אֲשֶׁר יִדְּבֶנּוּ לִבּוֹ״ — 'from every man whose heart moves him' — between the two first contributions, which were a half-shekel per head and were an absolute obligation upon every man of Israel, not dependent at all on the generosity of the donor's heart, since only the third contribution, of which it says ״זָהָב וָכֶסֶף וּנְחֹשֶׁת״, came as a free-will offering? And why does the latter use the term קִיחָה, which connotes compulsion, when a thing given freely involves no compulsion? And furthermore: in the first it says ״וְיִקְחוּ״ in the impersonal/passive form, while in the latter two it says ״תִּקְחוּ״ in the direct address form." "And what seems closest to me to say about this is that, since in the matter of the shekels there is a dispute among the Torah commentators — for some hold that Israel was not counted by head, but rather the shekels were counted in order to deliver them from the evil eye — and Maharai Abravanel disagrees with this opinion and says they were literally counted by head, and the shekels were given by way of charity, either before the census or after it, to atone for the soul, as is explained in his book on the portion of כִּי תִשָּׂא." "And behold, there is room for me to interpret the word ״יִדְּבֶנּוּ״ in two ways: either as the language of free-will offering, according to its plain sense, or the exact opposite — since it is not written ״יִנְדְּבֶנּוּ״ with a nun, there is room to interpret it as ״יִדְּוֶנּוּ״ with a vav, for the letters bet, mem, and peh interchange, especially with a rafeh bet which is pronounced like a vav. And this refers to one whose heart is sick (דָּוֶה) and aches over the giving. Therefore, in the two first contributions, which according to all opinions were an obligation upon every man — and even if he did not wish to contribute, the collectors could in any case take from him by force — therefore in those He put the language of קִיחָה before תְּרוּמָה: for through the collectors coming to collect from him by force, he comes to contribute; for the act of taking by the collectors causes the contribution to happen. But the third, which came as a free-will offering and involved no obligation at all — and if he did not wish to contribute, who could compel him? — therefore in it He put תְּרוּמָה before לְקִיחָה, to say that after he, of his own heart's generosity, made a pledge and contributed from his property according to his will, then the collectors may compel him and take from him by force what he has already pledged, whether verbally or by hand. Therefore it says ״וְזֹאת הַתְּרוּמָה אֲשֶׁר תִּקְחוּ מֵאִתָּם״, etc. — to say that after he has contributed, then ״תִּקְחוּ מֵאִתָּם״ — you shall take from them by force, if need be, when he regrets it." "This is the meaning of ״דַּבֵּר אֶל בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל וְיִקְחוּ לִי תְּרוּמָה״ — that they should appoint collectors who will take the contribution from each and every man, and before they demand from others, they themselves should give their own share, to establish themselves as honest first, and afterward take from others; and this is the force of the vav of ״וְיִקְחוּ״. But ״מֵאֵת כָּל אִישׁ אֲשֶׁר יִדְּבֶנּוּ לִבּוֹ״ — his heart aches over the giving, for he is a miserly man and does not give with good will, and there is reason to suspect that he may quarrel with the collectors — then ״תִּקְחוּ״, you yourselves — Moshe and Aharon or the princes of Israel — shall take, for they will surely show them deference and not contend with them over this. But if ״יִדְּבֶנּוּ״ means as ״יִנְדְּבֶנּוּ״ — i.e., the language of a generous heart — then it refers back to what was said above: ״וְיִקְחוּ לִי״ — the collectors — ״תְּרוּמָה מֵאֵת כָּל אִישׁ אֲשֶׁר יִדְּבֶנּוּ״ — whose heart is generous — and by implication we hear that what He said afterward, ״תִּקְחוּ אֶת תְּרוּמָתִי״, means 'you yourselves shall take,' speaking of one who is not generous of heart, for the reason already explained. However, from the fact that it says in the portion of וַיַּקְהֵל (Exodus 35:5) ״כֹּל נְדִיב לֵב יְבִיאֶהָ״ — 'whoever is of a generous heart shall bring it' — hear from this that here He speaks of one whose heart aches and is in pain, and from him you shall take by force. But one who is of a generous heart, he brings it of himself and there is no need to compel him. However, the term ״תִּקְחוּ״ stated regarding the third contribution, which comes as a free-will offering, has another matter within it — namely, that if distinguished collectors come to the donor, he will undoubtedly show them deference and donate more than if ordinary people came to him. For this reason the practice is to solicit donations in public — perhaps he will show them deference." "And according to Maharai A. — who explained that even the shekels, although they were an obligation, were nevertheless like charity and an atonement — the statement ״מֵאֵת כָּל אִישׁ אֲשֶׁר יִדְּבֶנּוּ לִבּוֹ״ would be the language of free-will offering in the literal sense, and refers both to what is above and to what is below, teaching that these two contributions as well depended upon the generosity of the donor's heart. But in the third contribution there was no need to mention this, for it is self-evident that it came as a free-will offering and not an obligation, since this was already intimated by the fact that He put תְּרוּמָה before קִיחָה. And because the two first contributions are an obligation and an atonement for the soul, as it is written ״לְכַפֵּר עַל נַפְשֹׁתֵיכֶם״ (Exodus 30:15), and it is written (Ezekiel 18:4) ״הֵן כָּל הַנְּפָשׁוֹת לִי הֵנָּה״ — 'Behold, all souls are Mine' — and by the grace of El, blessed be He, He takes a half-shekel per head in exchange for the soul — therefore in the first it says ״וְיִקְחוּ לִי״ and in the second ״תְּרוּמָתִי״, for the name of HaShem is called upon them. But the third, which is not an obligation — He did not attribute it to HaShem, blessed be He, but suspended it upon the donors, as it says ״וְזֹאת הַתְּרוּמָה אֲשֶׁר תִּקְחוּ מֵאִתָּם״, for it depends on them; and this is why it says ״מֵאִתָּם״ — it was not from HaShem that this was an obligation." "And behold, one may hear another explanation for why He attributed the first two donations to the Name, blessed be He, but not the third — for in every place where there is a quality of humility and lowliness among those below, there the might of the Holy One, blessed be He, is made manifest, for He dwells with the crushed and lowly of spirit. But in every place where there is a stirring of pride, the Holy One, blessed be He, does not wish to unite His name, blessed be He, there. Therefore the first two donations, in which every person's hand was equal — for 'the rich shall not increase and the poor shall not decrease' — and there was no room for anyone to boast over his fellow and say, 'My donation is greater than your donation' — those He attributed to the Name, blessed be He. For in the first one it is written ״וְיִקְחוּ לִי״ — 'for My name,' for we have already explained above at the beginning of parashat מִקֵּץ and in the verse ״בְּכָל הַמָּקוֹם אֲשֶׁר אַזְכִּיר אֶת שְׁמִי״ (Exodus 20:21) that all the letters of the great Name indicate the quality of humility — see there. And in the second one He said ״תְּרוּמָתִי״, 'My donation.' And so the thing made from these two donations resembles the donation itself: just as all were equal in it and there was no haughtiness for one over another, so too was made from it the single donation of the sockets. For all the sockets were equal, one like the other, and they were at the bottom of the entire structure like a threshold that is trodden upon, and they were the foundations of the entire building. For 'like them shall be their makers' — for just as the makers of that donation were equal in giving and there was no haughtiness for any one of them over his fellow, so too were made from them these sockets that indicate the quality of humility. For humility is the foundation of the building upon which the whole house of Israel stands firm, and they were called by the name 'sockets' (אֲדָנִים), which carries in its meaning the connotation of mastery and dominion (אַדְנוּת), because whoever makes himself like a threshold trodden upon below becomes a master above. For everyone who humbles himself, the Holy One, blessed be He, raises him up, and makes him a foundation and support for the entire building, like these sockets which were the foundation and support for the entire building. And likewise the word 'נָשִׂיא' ('prince') means that he carries others, for they all lean upon him." "And from the second donation they brought the communal offerings, which were also equal every day — one lamb in the morning and the second between the evenings — and it is written (Psalms 51:19) ״זִבְחֵי אֱלֹהִים רוּחַ נִשְׁבָּרָה״, meaning that the ultimate purpose of the sacrifices is to bring a person to a broken spirit. And therefore He attributed it also to Him, blessed be He, for it is written ״תִּקְחוּ אֶת תְּרוּמָתִי״, because the donation and what is made from it all indicate the quality of humility. Therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, said ״זוֹ הִיא תְּרוּמָתִי״. But the third donation, in which they were not all equal and they differed in less and more — for the rich man who gives much has room for haughtiness over the poor man, saying, 'I have a greater share in the house of our Elohim than the poor man, for I have multiplied silver and gold' — therefore from it were made all the sacred vessels, such as the Ark and the Menorah and the Table and the Altar and the rest of the vessels. And without doubt there was superiority for some over others: for the crown of the Ark corresponds to the crown of Torah, and the crown of the Altar corresponds to the crown of priesthood, and the crown of the Table corresponds to the crown of kingship. And even though according to the sanctity of the vessels the Holy One, blessed be He, should have attributed this donation to Him, blessed be He, nevertheless, since from the side of the collection of the donation there was an aspect of pride and haughtiness on the part of the one who gave much over the one who gave little, therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, did not attribute it to Him." "And with this I saw fit to resolve one difficulty: since He reckoned the donation in the order of their rank — gold and silver and bronze — if so, why does He count shoham stones and filling stones at the end, for the stones are without doubt more precious than gold? Even though we could answer and say that since the princes brought the shoham stones and the filling stones last, as Rashi cites (Exodus 35:27), therefore He mentioned their donation last — nevertheless, according to our approach as well, we may say that the Holy One, blessed be He, wished to show that the one who gives little and the one who gives much are all equal before Him, blessed be He. Therefore He mentioned the precious one at the end, so that the glory of the rich not be over the poor, saying, 'My donation is great and the verse counts it first.' And further, He mentioned their donation last, because the haughtiness of their heart brought them to the point that they volunteered last, saying, 'Let the public volunteer and whatever they lack, we will make up' — and all this is a way of pride and arrogance, to say, 'Our hands have the power to fill what they lack.' Therefore He mentioned their donation last, for everyone who exalts himself, the Holy One, blessed be He, humbles him. And not only that, but וְהַנְּשִׂאִם ('and the princes') is written deficient, missing the letter יוֹד — for only one letter of the great Name is engraved in the name 'נָשִׂיא', namely the יוֹד, and because they exalted themselves in their words, HaShem took this letter from their name, from His name, blessed be He, which indicates entirely the quality of humility, as explained in the verse (Exodus 20:21) ״בְּכָל הַמָּקוֹם אֲשֶׁר אַזְכִּיר אֶת שְׁמִי אָבֹא אֵלֶיךָ״. And this matter is a secret of the secrets of the Torah. And the form of the letter יוֹד also indicates humility and smallness."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {4 "Maharai A. expanded in the source text as מַהֲרִי״א; resolved as Maharai Abravanel per the abbreviation in comment [1]."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:23:27.002829497Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}